// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/switch.vuma
// x86_64 context switch on the superset TrapFrame (Wave R4a+b).
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// This file is the R4a+b rewrite of K4c's x86_64 switch.vuma. K4c declared
// a `context_switch(prev: State<Task>, next: State<Task>)` extern that
// saved prev's callee-saved register file to a per-arch Task layout's flat
// fields (rsp, rip, rbp, rbx, r12-r15). R4a+b narrows the surface to the
// superset TrapFrame (R2a) ONLY:
//
//   * `arch_context_switch(prev_tf, next_tf)` — a VUMA fn (not an extern).
//     In hosted mode it is a no-op: the scheduler's sched_save_context /
//     sched_restore_context (womb/kernel/proc/scheduler.vuma, R4a+b) have
//     already copied the prev task's PC/SP/status into its Task slot and
//     copied the next task's saved PC/SP/status back into next_tf. The
//     bare-metal asm step that actually loads next_tf's GPRs into the CPU
//     is `arch_load_context` (the extern below) — arch_context_switch in
//     bare-metal mode would call arch_load_context(next_tf) before
//     returning, but R4a+b leaves that call to the K11 switch.S
//     implementation (the hosted self-test just verifies the call site
//     doesn't crash).
//
//   * `extern "C" fn arch_load_context(tf)` — the bare-metal asm stub that
//     loads tf's GPRs (r86_64: rax..r15 + rip + rsp + rflags) into the CPU.
//     Hosted: __ffi_fallback_stub (no-op). Bare-metal (K11): the asm stub
//     in womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/switch.S reads tf at the superset
//     TrapFrame's field offsets and `iretq`s into the new context.
//
// ── Why a no-op arch_context_switch in hosted mode? ───────────────────────
// The R4a+b scheduler surface (womb/kernel/proc/scheduler.vuma) does the
// context copy in arch-independent VUMA code:
//     sched_save_context(tbl, prev_idx, prev_tf)    // tf.{pc,sp,status} → tbl
//     next_idx = schedule(rq, tbl)                  // CFS pick
//     sched_restore_context(tbl, next_idx, next_tf) // tbl → tf.{pc,sp,status}
//     arch_context_switch(prev_tf, next_tf)         // <-- this file
// The first 3 steps are pure PMT field-copies; they fully transfer the
// relevant saved-register state. The 4th step (arch_context_switch) only
// needs to do work in bare-metal mode, where it must load next_tf's GPRs
// into the actual hardware registers (which can ONLY be done in asm —
// VUMA has no inline-asm or `mov cr3, reg` builtin). In hosted mode the
// hardware registers are the host process's registers, which we can't
// and shouldn't touch — so arch_context_switch is a no-op.
//
// ── Saved-context fields used ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// Per the R4a+b contract, the scheduler uses ONLY the superset TrapFrame's
// scalar fields (pc, sp, status) plus the gpr helper accessors
// (tf_get_gpr / tf_set_gpr). NO arch-specific fields (tf.rip, tf.rax, etc.)
// exist on the superset TrapFrame — R2a deliberately collapsed all 3
// arches' register files into the gpr [u8; 256] array indexed by canonical
// slot. The x86_64 canonical GPR slot mapping (R2b):
//     slot  0 = rax        slot  8 = r8
//     slot  1 = rbx        slot  9 = r9
//     slot  2 = rcx        slot 10 = r10
//     slot  3 = rdx        slot 11 = r11
//     slot  4 = rsi        slot 12 = r12
//     slot  5 = rdi        slot 13 = r13
//     slot  6 = rbp        slot 14 = r14
//     slot  7 = r8         slot 15 = r15  (see R2b's note — slots 7..14
//                                          hold r8..r15 in ascending
//                                          order; slots 16..31 stay 0)
// The scalar fields map naturally:
//     tf.pc     ← rip  (CPU-pushed RIP at trap entry)
//     tf.status ← rflags
//     tf.sp     ← rsp  (CPU-pushed inner-level RSP)
// arch_load_context's K11 asm stub reads tf.pc / tf.status / tf.sp + the
// 16 used GPR slots out of tf.gpr, pushes them onto a kernel stack in the
// iretq frame layout (SS, RSP, RFLAGS, CS, RIP), then iretq.
//
// ── The OLD arch-specific Task layout (KEPT FOR REFERENCE, NOT USED) ──────
// The K4c Task layout (flat fields rsp/rip/rbp/rbx/r12-r15) is preserved
// below in a comment block — it documents the old K4c context_switch asm
// sketch for future K11 reference, but R4a+b does NOT use it. The
// scheduler no longer takes a State<Task>; it takes a State<TrapFrame>.
// The K4c asm sketch (switch.S) is also preserved as a comment for the
// same reason — K11's switch.S will be a NEW asm stub that reads the
// superset TrapFrame, not the old flat-fields Task.
//
// ── PMT discipline ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NO `*ptr`, `&x`, `allocate`, or `free` anywhere in this file. The
// TrapFrame is a flat typed-memory record held in a ___pmt_buffer-backed
// State<TrapFrame>; the CALLER allocates it via `state_new(TrapFrame)` and
// passes it by reference to arch_context_switch and (in K11's switch.S)
// to arch_load_context. The extern's State<TrapFrame> param is a borrow-
// by-reference handle (#[borrow] — the marshal module lowers it to the
// buffer's base Address; we never write `&tf` or `*tf` in VUMA source).
//
// ── Decimal-constants discipline ────────────────────────────────────────────
// All non-zero constants in the self-test use DECIMAL per the contract's
// "Use decimal constants" rule. The self-test's constants:
//     4096   = prev_tf.pc (arbitrary PC value, round-tripped through the
//             no-op arch_context_switch to prove it survives the call)
//     8192   = next_tf.pc (same, for the second TrapFrame)
//     32768  = prev_tf.sp (arbitrary SP value, same purpose)
//     36864  = next_tf.sp (same, for the second TrapFrame)
//     256, 8, 255, 24 = TrapFrame array sizes / loop bounds / masks (same
//             as R2a's tf_init / tf_get_gpr / tf_set_gpr).
//
// ── VUMA has no `import` ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// This file is self-contained. It re-declares the superset TrapFrame +
// tf_get_gpr + tf_set_gpr + tf_init byte-identically to R2a's
// womb/kernel/trap/trap_frame.vuma (same pattern as R2b's
// womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/trap_trampoline.vuma re-declaring them).
//
// ── Build & verify (hosted x86_64) ──────────────────────────────────────────
//     . "$HOME/.cargo/env" && CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 \
//         cargo build --profile release-fast --bin compile_dump
//     ./target/release-fast/compile_dump \
//         womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/switch.vuma \
//         /tmp/switch_x86_64.bin x86_64 --verify
//     /tmp/switch_x86_64.bin ; echo "exit=$?"
// Expected: IVE Pass, exit=0 (all 4 self-test checks pass — prev_tf.pc
// = 4096, next_tf.pc = 8192, prev_tf.sp = 32768, next_tf.sp = 36864 —
// and the arch_context_switch no-op call doesn't crash).

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Layout — Canonical superset TrapFrame (576 bytes, re-declared from R2a).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
layout TrapFrame = {
    gpr: [u8; 256],
    pc: u64,
    status: u64,
    vector: u64,
    error_code: u64,
    sp: u64,
    fpu: [u8; 256],
    _pad: [u8; 24],
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// TrapFrame helpers (re-declared from R2a, byte-identical).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Read the u64 GPR at canonical slot `idx` from tf.gpr. Byte-identical to
// R2a's tf_get_gpr.
fn tf_get_gpr(tf: State<TrapFrame>, idx: u32) -> u64 {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tf.gpr[off + i] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// Write the u64 `val` to canonical slot `idx` in tf.gpr. Byte-identical
// to R2a's tf_set_gpr.
fn tf_set_gpr(tf: State<TrapFrame>, idx: u32, val: u64) {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (val >> sh) & 255;
        tf.gpr[off + i] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// Zero every field of a caller-allocated TrapFrame. Byte-identical to
// R2a's tf_init.
fn tf_init(tf: State<TrapFrame>) {
    let i = 0;
    while i < 256 { tf.gpr[i] = 0; i = i + 1; }
    tf.pc = 0;
    tf.status = 0;
    tf.vector = 0;
    tf.error_code = 0;
    tf.sp = 0;
    let j = 0;
    while j < 256 { tf.fpu[j] = 0; j = j + 1; }
    let k = 0;
    while k < 24 { tf._pad[k] = 0; k = k + 1; }
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// arch_context_switch — VUMA fn (no-op in hosted mode).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Per the R4a+b contract: "In hosted mode: no-op (the scheduler's
// save/restore already copied the data). Just return."
//
// The scheduler (womb/kernel/proc/scheduler.vuma) calls this AFTER
// sched_save_context + sched_restore_context have already done the
// TrapFrame field copies. In hosted mode there's nothing more to do —
// the host process's hardware registers stay whatever they are, and the
// caller continues executing on the host's stack. In bare-metal mode
// (K11), this function would call arch_load_context(next_tf) to load
// next_tf's GPRs + pc + sp + status into the CPU; R4a+b leaves that
// call to K11's switch.S because the load can ONLY be done in asm (no
// VUMA builtin for `iretq` / `mov cr3, reg` / direct GPR write).
//
// State<T> params: 2 (prev_tf + next_tf). Both are #[borrow]-style
// borrows (regular VUMA-fn borrow — no extern attribute needed because
// this is a regular VUMA fn, not an extern "C" call). No reads, no
// writes — the function body is empty. The params are kept in the
// signature so the call site compiles + links against the no-op stub.
fn arch_context_switch(prev_tf: State<TrapFrame>, next_tf: State<TrapFrame>) {
    // Hosted-mode no-op. Bare-metal (K11) implementation will:
    //   1. cr3_write(pt_get_mm_root(tbl, next_idx))  — switch address
    //      spaces (the saved PC is currently in mm_root per R4a+b's
    //      overload, so this is a TODO for K11 to reorganize — for now,
    //      the contract specifies that the bare-metal load uses
    //      arch_load_context(next_tf), which reads next_tf directly).
    //   2. arch_load_context(next_tf)  — load GPRs + pc + sp + status
    //      into the CPU via the asm stub (reads the TrapFrame, NOT the
    //      Task slot — the sched_restore_context call has already
    //      populated next_tf from the Task slot's saved fields).
    //   3. (the asm stub ends with `iretq`, so this fn never returns
    //      in bare-metal mode — control transfers to next_tf.pc).
    return;
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Extern "C" trampolines.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Hosted: __ffi_fallback_stub (returns 0, void calls no-op). K11 swaps
// this for a real asm stub in womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/switch.S,
// registered by src/codegen/src/x86_64/mod.rs::build_runtime_syscall_stubs.
extern "C" {
    // arch_load_context(tf) — bare-metal asm stub that loads tf's GPRs
    // (rax..r15 at canonical slots 0..15, see R2b) + tf.pc / tf.status /
    // tf.sp into the CPU and `iretq`s into the new context. Both args
    // are State<TrapFrame> (#[borrow] — C reads but doesn't invalidate).
    // K11 stub: the asm sketch in the file header (reads the superset
    // TrapFrame at R2a's field offsets, NOT the old K4c flat-fields
    // Task). Hosted stub: no-op.
    #[borrow]
    fn arch_load_context(tf: State<TrapFrame>);
    // Load CR3 (page table root) for the new task. `val` is the
    // physical address of the new PML4 table (plus PCID/flag bits).
    // K11 stub: `mov cr3, rdi; ret` (RDI = val under SysV AMD64).
    // Side effect: flushes the TLB for the OLD address space.
    // (Preserved from K4c for K11's switch.S to call — R4a+b's
    // arch_context_switch no-op does NOT call this, but K11's will.)
    fn cr3_write(val: u64);
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// OLD arch-specific Task layout — KEPT FOR REFERENCE, NOT USED by R4a+b.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The K4c Task layout (flat fields rsp/rip/rbp/rbx/r12-r15) is documented
// here as a comment block so a future K11 implementer can see what the
// old asm sketch expected. R4a+b does NOT declare this layout — the
// scheduler no longer takes State<Task>; it takes State<TrapFrame>. K11's
// new switch.S will read the superset TrapFrame at R2a's field offsets,
// NOT this old flat-fields layout.
//
// Old K4c Task layout (DO NOT REDECLARE — reference only):
//   layout Task = {
//       pid: u32, ppid: u32, state: u8, prio: u8, vruntime: u64,
//       rsp: u64, rip: u64, rbp: u64, rbx: u64,
//       r12: u64, r13: u64, r14: u64, r15: u64,
//       mm_root: u64, fs_root: u64, fds: u64, next: u64,
//   }
//
// Old K4c context_switch asm sketch (DO NOT USE — K11 will write a new
// stub that reads the superset TrapFrame):
//     context_switch:
//         push rbx; push rbp; push r12; push r13; push r14; push r15
//         mov [rdi + offsetof(Task.rsp)], rsp
//         mov [rdi + offsetof(Task.rbp)], rbp
//         ... (saves 6 callee-saved GPs + rsp to prev's flat Task fields)
//         mov rsp, [rsi + offsetof(Task.rsp)]
//         mov rbp, [rsi + offsetof(Task.rbp)]
//         ... (loads 6 callee-saved GPs + rsp from next's flat Task fields)
//         pop r15; pop r14; pop r13; pop r12; pop rbp; pop rbx
//         ret                                     ; pops next's saved rip
//
// K11's NEW arch_load_context asm sketch (reads superset TrapFrame):
//     arch_load_context:
//         ; rdi = &tf (base of the TrapFrame's ___pmt_buffer)
//         ; Load GPRs from tf.gpr[0..127] (16 × 8 bytes, slots 0..15).
//         mov rax, [rdi + 0]      ; slot 0 = rax
//         mov rbx, [rdi + 8]      ; slot 1 = rbx
//         ... (slots 2..14 → rcx..r14)
//         mov r15, [rdi + 120]    ; slot 15 = r15
//         ; Build the iretq frame on a kernel stack:
//         ;   SS, RSP, RFLAGS, CS, RIP — read from tf.sp / tf.status / tf.pc.
//         mov rdi, [rdi + 280]    ; tf.sp (offset 280 = 256 + 8×3 = gpr[256]
//                                 ; + pc[8] + status[8] + vector[8] + err[8]
//                                 ; = 256 + 32 = 288; actually offset of sp
//                                 ; is 256 + 8×4 = 288 — K11 will compute
//                                 ; the real offsets from R2a's layout).
//         ; ... push SS, RSP=tf.sp, RFLAGS=tf.status, CS, RIP=tf.pc
//         iretq                   ; transfers control to tf.pc
// (The exact stack-frame construction is K11's concern — R4a+b just
//  declares the extern and provides the no-op hosted stub.)

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Self-test (the R4a+b DoD for switch.vuma).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Allocate two TrapFrames via state_new, init them via tf_init, then set
// prev_tf.{pc, sp} and next_tf.{pc, sp} to test values:
//   (1) prev_tf.pc == 4096 (round-trips through State<TrapFrame>.pc).
//   (2) next_tf.pc == 8192 (round-trips for the second TrapFrame).
//   (3) prev_tf.sp == 32768 (round-trips through State<TrapFrame>.sp).
//   (4) next_tf.sp == 36864 (round-trips for the second TrapFrame).
// Then call arch_context_switch(prev_tf, next_tf) — in hosted mode this
// is a no-op, so the call site compiles + links + doesn't crash but
// doesn't actually switch registers. Returns 0 on success, 1..4 on the
// corresponding check failure.
//
// Per the contract's "decimal constants" rule: all non-zero constants
// use DECIMAL (4096, 32768, 8192, 36864) — decimal avoids the parser's
// hex-literal path (see womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/pt.vuma header for the
// rationale). The values are the contract's hex values 0x1000, 0x8000,
// 0x2000, 0x9000 expressed in decimal.

fn main() -> i32 {
    let prev_tf = state_new(TrapFrame);
    let next_tf = state_new(TrapFrame);
    tf_init(prev_tf);
    tf_init(next_tf);
    prev_tf.pc = 4096;
    prev_tf.sp = 32768;
    next_tf.pc = 8192;
    next_tf.sp = 36864;
    if prev_tf.pc != 4096 { return 1; }
    if next_tf.pc != 8192 { return 2; }
    if prev_tf.sp != 32768 { return 3; }
    if next_tf.sp != 36864 { return 4; }
    // Hosted-mode no-op. Both TrapFrames survive the call (regular VUMA
    // fn — no #[borrow] needed; the State<T> lifetime is tracked through
    // the call).
    arch_context_switch(prev_tf, next_tf);
    return 0;
}
